    "result_text": "I implemented `answer()` in `app/chat.py`, and all 7 tests pass (`pytest -q`), including 5 new ones.\n\n**How it works** (`app/chat.py`):\n- Each attempt runs the full streamed request from the start and joins the text. If an attempt drops partway through, its partial text is thrown away, so only a completed attempt's text is returned.\n- If a connection fails or drops (`APIConnectionError`), it logs a WARNING on `app.chat` and tries again. After `ATTEMPTS` (3) failures it raises `UpstreamUnavailable`.\n- A timeout (`APITimeoutError`) raises `UpstreamTimeout` right away with no retry. It has to be checked first because the SDK makes it a kind of `APIConnectionError`, so otherwise timeouts would be retried.\n- **Log message:** the OpenAI SDK replaces the network error's message with its own generic \"Connection error.\" / \"Request timed out.\", both on connect and while streaming. The original error is kept as the exception's `__cause__`, so a small helper, `_network_error_message`, follows that chain to the innermost error. That lets the warning show text like \"peer closed connection without sending complete message body\" or \"connection reset by peer\".\n- `history` is turned into a list once at the start. Otherwise a one-shot iterable (like a generator) would be used up by the first attempt, and retries would silently drop the conversation history.\n- The client is used as given: its own `max_retries` is left alone.\n- Other errors, such as HTTP 4xx/5xx responses (`APIStatusError`), are not retried and pass through unchanged. They aren't connection failures, so I read them as outside your spec. Tell me if you want 5xx retried too.\n\n**Tests** (`tests/test_chat.py`) use a real `OpenAI` client over a fake httpx2 transport, so the SDK's real error wrapping is tested. They cover:\n- a normal answer on the first try\n- a connect error, then a stream that drops partway, then success: only the final text comes back, each warning contains the underlying error's message, and the history re"
  },
